The pgvector README says it in one line: adding an approximate index "trades some recall for speed", and your results will differ from an exact scan once it is there. The default HNSW search setting, hnsw.ef_search, is 40. If your RAG app runs on Postgres and nobody on the team has ever changed hnsw.ef_search, then 40 is the number deciding which chunks your model gets to read.
Qdrant has the same dial under a different name, hnsw_ef, passed per request. Different databases, same algorithm, same trade.
You can read about that trade, or you can build the index yourself and watch it happen. Below, you write a small HNSW in TypeScript, index 50,000 random vectors in Node, and measure recall@10 against brute force at efSearch 10, 40, 100 and 200. The script is deterministic and short enough to read in one sitting, and every number it prints comes from your machine.
HNSW comes from a 2016 paper by Malkov and Yashunin on approximate nearest neighbor search with Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs. Strip away the proofs and three ideas remain.
Layers. Every vector lives on layer 0. A random few also live on layer 1, fewer still on layer 2, and so on. The paper draws each element's top layer as floor(-ln(uniform) * mL) and suggests mL = 1/ln(M), where M is the number of links per node. That gives you something shaped like a skip list: a sparse top for long jumps, a dense bottom for precision.
Greedy descent. A query starts at a single entry point on the top layer and walks to whichever neighbor is closer, until nothing is closer. Then it drops one layer and repeats from there. In the paper's search algorithm, the upper layers run with ef = 1: one candidate, pure greed.
A beam on layer 0. On the bottom layer the search keeps a list of the ef best nodes found so far and keeps expanding the nearest unexpanded one. It stops when the nearest unexpanded candidate is farther than the worst node in the list. That ef is efSearch. A bigger beam explores more of the graph, finds more true neighbors, and computes more distances.

HNSW in one picture: long hops on the sparse top layers, a wide beam on the dense bottom layer.
Building the graph reuses the same search. To insert a vector you search for its neighbors with a beam of efConstruction, link it to up to M of them on each of its layers, and trim any neighbor that now has too many links. The paper recommends 2·M links on layer 0; this version caps every other layer at M.
Everything below goes in one file, hnsw.ts: append each code block in order. Node runs .ts files directly since v22.18.0 and v23.6.0 by stripping the types, so there is no build step. The code avoids enums and parameter properties, which type stripping does not support.
// hnsw.ts: run with `node hnsw.ts` (Node 22.18+)
type Vec = Float32Array;
type Hit = { id: number; d: number };

// mulberry32: tiny seeded PRNG, same data every run
function mulberry32(seed: number): () => number {
  let a = seed >>> 0;
  return () => {
    a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0;
    let t = a;
    t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);
    t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);
    return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
  };
}

function randomUnit(dim: number, rand: () => number): Vec {
  const v = new Float32Array(dim);
  let norm = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < dim; i++) {
    v[i] = rand() * 2 - 1;
    norm += v[i] * v[i];
  }
  norm = Math.sqrt(norm);
  for (let i = 0; i < dim; i++) v[i] /= norm;
  return v;
}

The generator is seeded, so every machine builds the same 50,000 vectors and two readers' runs are comparable. Next come the distance function and a sorted insert.
// squared L2; on unit vectors it ranks like cosine
function dist(a: Vec, b: Vec): number {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    const d = a[i] - b[i];
    s += d * d;
  }
  return s;
}

// keep `arr` sorted by distance, nearest first
function insertSorted(arr: Hit[], h: Hit): void {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (arr[mid].d < h.d) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid;
  }
  arr.splice(lo, 0, h);
}

The vectors are normalized, so squared Euclidean distance orders results the same way cosine similarity does: for unit vectors, |a - b|² = 2 - 2·cos(a, b). A sorted array stands in for the two priority queues the paper uses. It is slower than a heap, but at beam sizes of a few hundred it stays readable and correct, which matters more here.
Brute force is the answer key. It scores every vector and keeps the k closest.
function bruteForce(data: Vec[], q: Vec, k: number): number[] {
  const best: Hit[] = [];
  for (let id = 0; id < data.length; id++) {
    const d = dist(q, data[id]);
    if (best.length < k || d < best[best.length - 1].d) {
      insertSorted(best, { id, d });
      if (best.length > k) best.pop();
    }
  }
  return best.map((h) => h.id);
}

searchLayer is the paper's Algorithm 2, selectNeighbors is its neighbor heuristic (Algorithm 4, without the optional extensions), and insert and search follow Algorithms 1 and 5.
class HNSW {
  M: number;
  M0: number;
  efC: number;
  mL: number;
  rand: () => number;
  vecs: Vec[] = [];
  links: number[][][] = []; // links[node][layer] -> ids
  entry = -1;
  maxLayer = -1;

  constructor(M: number, efC: number, rand: () => number) {
    this.M = M;
    this.M0 = 2 * M; // layer 0 gets twice the links
    this.efC = efC;
    this.mL = 1 / Math.log(M);
    this.rand = rand;
  }

  // beam search on one layer, returns the ef best
  searchLayer(q: Vec, eps: number[], ef: number, lc: number) {
    const seen = new Set<number>(eps);
    const cand: Hit[] = [];
    const best: Hit[] = [];
    for (const id of eps) {
      const h = { id, d: dist(q, this.vecs[id]) };
      insertSorted(cand, h);
      insertSorted(best, h);
    }
    while (cand.length > 0) {
      const c = cand.shift()!;
      if (c.d > best[best.length - 1].d) break;
      for (const n of this.links[c.id][lc]) {
        if (seen.has(n)) continue;
        seen.add(n);
        const d = dist(q, this.vecs[n]);
        const worst = best[best.length - 1].d;
        if (best.length < ef || d < worst) {
          insertSorted(cand, { id: n, d });
          insertSorted(best, { id: n, d });
          if (best.length > ef) best.pop();
        }
      }
    }
    return best;
  }

  // keep a candidate only if it is closer to the base
  // than to every neighbor already kept
  selectNeighbors(cands: Hit[], m: number): number[] {
    const kept: Hit[] = [];
    for (const c of cands) {
      if (kept.length >= m) break;
      let ok = true;
      for (const k of kept) {
        if (dist(this.vecs[c.id], this.vecs[k.id]) < c.d) {
          ok = false;
          break;
        }
      }
      if (ok) kept.push(c);
    }
    return kept.map((h) => h.id);
  }

  shrink(n: number, lc: number, cap: number): void {
    const base = this.vecs[n];
    const hits = this.links[n][lc]
      .map((id) => ({ id, d: dist(base, this.vecs[id]) }))
      .sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d);
    this.links[n][lc] = this.selectNeighbors(hits, cap);
  }

  insert(v: Vec): void {
    const id = this.vecs.length;
    const level = Math.floor(-Math.log(1 - this.rand()) * this.mL);
    this.vecs.push(v);
    const layers: number[][] = [];
    for (let l = 0; l <= level; l++) layers.push([]);
    this.links.push(layers);
    if (this.entry < 0) {
      this.entry = id;
      this.maxLayer = level;
      return;
    }
    let ep = [this.entry];
    for (let lc = this.maxLayer; lc > level; lc--) {
      ep = [this.searchLayer(v, ep, 1, lc)[0].id];
    }
    for (let lc = Math.min(level, this.maxLayer); lc >= 0; lc--) {
      const found = this.searchLayer(v, ep, this.efC, lc);
      const nbrs = this.selectNeighbors(found, this.M);
      this.links[id][lc] = nbrs;
      const cap = lc === 0 ? this.M0 : this.M;
      for (const n of nbrs) {
        this.links[n][lc].push(id);
        if (this.links[n][lc].length > cap) {
          this.shrink(n, lc, cap);
        }
      }
      ep = found.map((h) => h.id);
    }
    if (level > this.maxLayer) {
      this.maxLayer = level;
      this.entry = id;
    }
  }

  search(q: Vec, k: number, ef: number): number[] {
    let ep = [this.entry];
    for (let lc = this.maxLayer; lc > 0; lc--) {
      ep = [this.searchLayer(q, ep, 1, lc)[0].id];
    }
    const found = this.searchLayer(q, ep, Math.max(ef, k), 0);
    return found.slice(0, k).map((h) => h.id);
  }
}

1 - this.rand() keeps the logarithm away from ln(0), because the generator can return 0 but never 1. search also clamps the beam to at least k, matching the hnswlib parameter notes: ef "cannot be set lower than the number of queried nearest neighbors k".
The neighbor heuristic is what keeps the graph navigable. Linking to the M closest points sounds right, but in clustered data the links stay inside each cluster and the search gets stuck at cluster boundaries. The paper's figure 7 shows this: the simple rule stalls at low recall on clustered data while the heuristic, with its candidate-extension option on, does not.
Append this to the same file. It builds the index with M = 16 and efConstruction = 64, which are pgvector's defaults, computes exact answers by brute force, then sweeps efSearch.
const N = 50_000;
const DIM = 64;
const Q = 500;
const K = 10;

const rand = mulberry32(42);
const data: Vec[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) data.push(randomUnit(DIM, rand));
const queries: Vec[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < Q; i++) queries.push(randomUnit(DIM, rand));

let t0 = performance.now();
const index = new HNSW(16, 64, rand); // pgvector defaults
for (const v of data) index.insert(v);
const buildS = (performance.now() - t0) / 1000;
console.log(`build: ${buildS.toFixed(1)} s`);
console.log(`top layer: ${index.maxLayer}`);

t0 = performance.now();
const truth = queries.map((q) => bruteForce(data, q, K));
const bfMs = (performance.now() - t0) / Q;
console.log(`brute force: ${bfMs.toFixed(2)} ms/query`);

With the exact answers in hand, the last block sweeps efSearch and scores each setting against them.
for (const ef of [10, 40, 100, 200]) {
  const t = performance.now();
  const got = queries.map((q) => index.search(q, K, ef));
  const ms = (performance.now() - t) / Q;
  let hits = 0;
  got.forEach((ids, i) => {
    const want = new Set(truth[i]);
    for (const id of ids) if (want.has(id)) hits++;
  });
  const recall = hits / (Q * K);
  console.log(
    `efSearch=${ef}  recall@10=${recall.toFixed(3)}  ` +
      `${ms.toFixed(3)} ms/query`,
  );
}

Run it with node hnsw.ts. The build is the slow part: 50,000 inserts, each one a beam search in plain JavaScript with no SIMD. If you want a faster first run, drop N to 10,000.
Recall@10 is the share of the true ten nearest neighbors that HNSW returned, averaged over 500 queries. The timer wraps only the search calls, so the recall bookkeeping does not leak into the per-query time.

efSearch is a dial between finding more of the true neighbors and answering sooner.
I'm not printing a results table here, because the only numbers worth trusting are the ones your run prints. What the docs predict is the direction. pgvector says a higher ef_search gives "better recall" at the cost of speed, and hnswlib says "higher ef leads to more accurate but slower search". Your run will show by how much on your hardware, and whether the step from 40 to 100 buys you more than the step from 100 to 200.
Random vectors are the hard case. Uniform random data has no clusters, and its intrinsic dimension is close to its nominal one. A 2024 paper by Elliott and Clark links HNSW recall to the intrinsic dimensionality of the data and to insertion order, and a Marqo post on HNSW recall notes that clustered data "tends to produce higher recall than uniformly distributed data". Real text embeddings have structure, so treat your random-data recall as a pessimistic floor. Try DIM = 16 and DIM = 256 (pair the second with a smaller N, since every distance costs four times as much) and watch the same efSearch buy very different recall.
The brute-force line is your baseline. At 50,000 vectors an exact scan is cheap, and pgvector without an index does exactly that with perfect recall. HNSW starts paying for itself as N grows, because brute force scales with the corpus and the graph search, per the paper, scales roughly logarithmically.
Filters make a small beam smaller. pgvector applies WHERE filters after the index scan. Its README spells out the consequence: "If a condition matches 10% of rows, with HNSW and the default hnsw.ef_search of 40, only 4 rows will match on average." That is a top-10 query returning 4 rows. Since 0.8.0, pgvector has iterative index scans that keep scanning until enough rows pass the filter.
Turning the dial in production. In Postgres it is one setting, scoped to a transaction if you want:
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL hnsw.ef_search = 100;
SELECT id FROM chunks ORDER BY embedding <=> $1 LIMIT 10;
COMMIT;

In Qdrant you pass it per search as "params": { "hnsw_ef": 128 }, and the indexing docs say ef defaults to the ef_construct value, which is 100 out of the box. Both stores also let you skip the index for an exact answer: pgvector with SET LOCAL enable_indexscan = off inside a transaction, which its README suggests for monitoring recall, and Qdrant with "exact": true. That exact path is how you build your own recall test on real data. Take a few hundred real queries, get exact top-10s once, then compare what your production setting returns.
Real implementations add what this one file skips: deletes and updates, concurrent inserts, storage on disk pages, quantized vectors, and SIMD distance kernels. The paper also has two optional switches on the neighbor heuristic, extending candidates and keeping pruned connections, that this version leaves off. To see why the heuristic is there at all, generate clustered vectors instead of calling randomUnit, swap selectNeighbors for a plain slice of the M closest candidates, and compare recall at the same efSearch.
Every RAG answer starts with a retrieval step, and that step is an approximate search with a recall number nobody wrote down. AI That Reads is the book in the series about that layer, in TypeScript: embeddings, vector search, chunking and grounding answers in your own documents, with the measuring habits that tell you when retrieval is the part that's failing.

It is book 2 of five in AI in TypeScript, from your first LLM call to agents in production: the whole series is here.

Geofencing is one of those features that sells itself. Your app notices when the user walks into the office and switches their phone to silent, then restores the ringer when they leave. No taps, no remembering — the app just knows where it is.
I know this pattern well because it is the core of Smart Silent, my Android app that automatically switches phone sound profiles based on scheduled times or geographic location. Under the hood it is battery-efficient geofencing: location-based profile switching that keeps battery usage under 1.2 percent per 24 hours, backed by deep OS-level controls for calls, media, and notification volumes.
The reason most geofencing implementations get deleted is not accuracy — it is battery life. A geofence feature that keeps the GPS radio hot all day is a feature users switch off, and a switched-off feature might as well not exist. Here are the five engineering rules that make geofencing in Flutter reliable and cheap enough to leave on permanently.
Both mobile operating systems already solved battery-efficient geofencing, and your job as a Flutter developer is mostly to not fight them.
On Android, the GeofencingClient registers geofences with the fused location provider. The OS batches location requests across every app on the device, so your 100-meter office geofence usually costs your app nothing extra — the radio wakes are shared with everything else running. Android supports up to 100 geofences per app and delivers three transition types: enter, exit, and dwell (the user has stayed inside for a configured time).
On iOS, region monitoring with circular regions works the same way at a conceptual level. The system can monitor your regions even when the app is suspended or terminated, waking it in the background to deliver enter and exit events. iOS limits an app to 20 simultaneously monitored regions, so iOS designs must register only the regions near the user and re-register as they move.
The single most important takeaway: never replace these native APIs with your own GPS polling loop. Polling the current position on a timer keeps the GPS radio awake and is the number one reason geofence features drain batteries.
Geofence radius is not just a UX decision — it is a power decision. Large geofences (100 meters and up) can be served by the fused location provider using low-power WiFi and cell-tower positioning. Small geofences (20 meters) force the system to wake the GPS chip to get fine fixes, which is dramatically more expensive.
As a rule of thumb, make 100 meters your minimum practical radius unless the use case truly demands otherwise. An office auto-mute feature does not need to know which floor the user is on — it needs to know they arrived at the office campus. If a product designer asks for a 15-meter geofence around a store entrance, push back and propose a larger zone with a dwell requirement instead. I explored how these power tradeoffs compound across a whole app in my guide on mobile battery optimization: https://www.uzairxdev.online/blog/graphql-vs-rest-mobile-battery
GPS positions drift. A phone sitting on a desk near the edge of a geofence will report positions that flicker in and out of the boundary, and naive code will fire enter, exit, enter, exit in a loop. This is not just annoying — every false transition wakes your app and costs power.
The fix is a small state machine with a dwell requirement: only fire a transition after the new state persists for a minimum duration. Here is a compact implementation using the geolocator package:
\`dart
/// Confirms geofence transitions only after the new state persists,
bool _inside = false;
GeofenceMonitor({
/// Returns 'enter' or 'exit' on a confirmed transition, null otherwise.
if (nowInside == _inside) {
  _crossedAt = null;
  return null;
}

_crossedAt ??= now;
if (now.difference(_crossedAt!) < dwellTime) {
  return null; // Still unconfirmed - wait out the drift.
}

_inside = nowInside;
_crossedAt = null;
return _inside ? 'enter' : 'exit';

}
}
\
Native geofence APIs have this concept built in — Android supports a loitering delay when registering a geofence, and iOS lets you require the device to be inside a region for a minimum time before notifying. Use the platform delay when registering the geofence, and keep a debounce layer in Dart for anything you compute manually.
Geofencing only matters when the user is not looking at your app, which means you need background location access — the most suspicious permission an app can ask for. Getting this wrong is how geofence features ship and never get enabled.
On Android 10, background location ("Allow all the time") became a separate request from foreground location. On Android 11 and later, the system deliberately refuses to show it in a dialog — the user must grant it in Settings. That means your permission flow must first request foreground access, then explain exactly why the feature needs background access, and then deep-link the user to Settings. Requesting background permission in an onboarding flow, before the user has even tried the feature, will convert at close to zero.
On iOS, the flow is similar: request "While Using", then upgrade to "Always" only at the moment the user enables the geofence feature, with purpose strings that say what the feature does in plain language.
A minimal permission helper looks like this:
\`dart
LocationPermission permission = await Geolocator.checkPermission();
\
Remember: on Android, even after this succeeds with whileInUse, background geofencing still needs the separate "Allow all the time" grant, which you must guide the user to in Settings. Do not ask for it until the feature is in front of them.
A geofence transition handler should do the smallest possible work: flip a local flag, update a scheduled profile, post a notification. That is it. If you fire local notifications on transitions, make sure that path is lean too — my full notifications setup guide covers the efficient route: https://www.uzairxdev.online/blog/flutter-firebase-push-notifications-guide
What kills the battery budget is everything developers stack on top of the transition — a network sync on every enter and exit, a database compaction, a full data refresh that pulls from the server. In a place with spotty reception, a user crossing the boundary repeatedly can trigger dozens of sync attempts, each one keeping the cellular radio awake.
Instead, treat the transition as a signal and defer the heavy work. Update local state immediately, then let WorkManager on Android or a background refresh task on iOS pick up any network sync under its own power-efficient schedule, with exponential backoff if it fails. I cover this deferral pattern in more detail in my offline-first architecture guide: https://www.uzairxdev.online/blog/offline-first-architecture-crdt-mobile
Geofences are hard to test by walking around the block, and you should not have to. The Android emulator's extended controls let you script location routes, and the iOS simulator accepts GPX files that simulate movement — including entering and leaving your geofence coordinates. Test the full path: registration, enter with dwell, exit, and what happens when location services are disabled mid-monitoring.
Then do one real-world test in a release build. Debug builds keep location services and logging far more active than production, so battery numbers measured in debug mode are meaningless. One afternoon walk with the release build will show you the real transition latency and confirm your dwell settings are sane.
Smart Silent puts all five rules together. It combines a scheduling system for recurring silent periods — meetings, sleep, prayer times — with battery-efficient geofencing for location-based profile switching, using deep OS-level controls to manage media, calls, and notification volumes accurately. When the event ends or the user leaves the area, profiles revert to normal automatically. The result is geofence accuracy you can rely on at under 1.2 percent battery cost per 24 hours.
If you are building anything location-aware, you can see how the app presents these features in the Smart Silent case study: https://www.uzairxdev.online/projects/smart-silent
Geofencing done right is boring in the best way: the OS does the heavy lifting, your radius is generous, transitions are debounced, permissions are earned in context, and handlers do almost nothing. The battery budget of a well-built geofence feature is close to zero because most of the cost is shared with location work the phone was already doing.
Get any one of these wrong and the feature still works in the demo — it just quietly becomes the reason users uninstall the app. Build for the battery, and the feature gets to keep existing.
